{"name":"Kiln Clay","hex":"#c6bea9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kiln","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An oven or furnace or a heated chamber, for the purpose of hardening, burning, calcining or drying anything; for example, firing ceramics, curing or preserving tobacco, or drying grain."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bake in a kiln; to fire."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kiln"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kiln","blocks":[{"text":"A kiln is a thermally insulated chamber, a type of oven, that produces temperatures sufficient to complete some process, such as hardening, drying, or chemical changes. Kilns have been used for millennia to turn objects made from clay into pottery, tiles and bricks. Various industries use rotary kilns for pyroprocessing and to transform many other materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Charcoal_Kilns%2C_California.JPG/330px-Charcoal_Kilns%2C_California.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiln","wikidata":"Q1724341"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay","wikidata":"Q42302"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Indian_brick_kiln.jpg/500px-Indian_brick_kiln.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Indian_brick_kiln.jpg/1280px-Indian_brick_kiln.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Indian_brick_kiln.jpg/500px-Indian_brick_kiln.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian brick kiln","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndian_brick_kiln.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Hop_kiln.png/500px-Hop_kiln.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Hop_kiln.png/500px-Hop_kiln.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hops kiln","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHop_kiln.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Farnham_Pottery%2C_Wrecclesham_-_bottle_kiln.jpg/500px-Farnham_Pottery%2C_Wrecclesham_-_bottle_kiln.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Farnham_Pottery%2C_Wrecclesham_-_bottle_kiln.jpg/500px-Farnham_Pottery%2C_Wrecclesham_-_bottle_kiln.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Farnham Pottery, Wrecclesham, Surrey with the preserved bottle kiln on the right of photo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFarnham_Pottery%2C_Wrecclesham_-_bottle_kiln.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Fi